Q: Access outer this from inner class in Scala A: <p>There are two ways.</p>
<h2>Using self</h2>
<pre><code>class Outer {
  self =&gt;
  def f = &quot;Outer&quot;
  class Inner {
    def f = &quot;Inner&quot;

    def outer = self.f
  }
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Using qualified this</h2>
<pre><code>class Outer {
  self =&gt;
  def f = &quot;Outer&quot;
  class Inner {
    def f = &quot;Inner&quot;

    def outer = Outer.this.f
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: WinUI 3 unpackaged self-contained app works in Debug/Release but crashes after Publish (0xc000027b in Microsoft.UI.Xaml.dll) A: <p>The issue was resolved by adding these project properties:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;WindowsAppSDKSelfContained&gt;true&lt;/WindowsAppSDKSelfContained&gt;
&lt;WindowsPackageType&gt;None&lt;/WindowsPackageType&gt;
&lt;EnableMsixTooling&gt;true&lt;/EnableMsixTooling&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and publishing with:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;SelfContained&gt;true&lt;/SelfContained&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>These settings were enough to successfully publish and run an unpackaged WinUI 3 self-contained application.</p>

Q: How to mock next/navigation in Vitest? A: <p>If you don't wish to use <a href="https://github.com/scottrippey/next-router-mock" rel="nofollow noreferrer">next-router-mock</a> or can't for some reason, the following worked well for me using Next.js 16:</p>
<p>Define your mock module somewhere like <code>__mocks__/next/navigation.ts</code>, for example like this (feel free to change it or extend as you find necessary):</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import type { NextRouter } from &quot;next/router&quot;;
import { vi } from &quot;vitest&quot;;

export const mockRouter: Partial&lt;NextRouter&gt; = {
  push: vi.fn(),
  replace: vi.fn(),
  reload: vi.fn(),
  back: vi.fn(),
  forward: vi.fn(),
  prefetch: vi.fn(),
};

export const useRouter = () =&gt; mockRouter;

export const useParams = vi.fn();
</code></pre>
<p>Then add a <code>test.alias</code> into <code>vitest.config.ts</code> , for example like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import path from &quot;node:path&quot;;

export default {
  test: {
    alias: {
      &quot;next/navigation&quot;: path.resolve(__dirname, &quot;__mocks__/next/navigation.ts&quot;),
    },
  },
};
</code></pre>
<p>Note: Vitest requires absolute pathname to the mock module file to prevent duplicate modules.</p>
<p>Then you can also test if router functions were invoked:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { mockRouter } from &quot;@/__mocks__/next/navigation&quot;;

expect(mockRouter.push).toHaveBeenCalledWith(&quot;/some-page&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>Note2: This answer is based on <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79896746/32424763">https://stackoverflow.com/a/79896746/32424763</a> but clarifies that it isn't <code>resolve.alias</code>, calculates the full path name, and adds example usage.</p>

Q: quickly adding email address for .NetCore MailKit smtp utility A: <p>If you can modify the <code>EmailAddress</code> class then the easiest change would be to make a class constructor with parameters for name and email e.g.</p>

<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class EmailAddress
{
    public EmailAddress() { }

    public EmailAddress(name, address) 
    { 
        Name = name;
        Address = address;  
    }
    // ...
</code></pre>

<p>Then you can create the new email address and add it directly to the list of addresses. e.g.</p>

<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>newMessage.ToAddresses.Add(new EmailAddress("Ken Watanabe", "kw100@xxxx.com"));
newMessage.ToAddresses.Add(new EmailAddress("Ryosuke Yamada", "ry1010104@yyyy.com"));
</code></pre>
